-- title:
--   Finite generation of the rational part of the Eisenstein quotient
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $p$ which is prime (as a `Fact` instance). Let $J =$ `JZero p` be the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the level-$p$ modular function field after base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, carrying its natural action of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, realised as the group of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms `AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ`. Two hypotheses are assumed. First, `hcomm : HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar p`, which says that the divisorial Hecke endomorphisms `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ` of $J$, one for each prime $\ell$, commute pairwise; this is exactly what makes the `HeckeAlg`-module structure `heckeModuleBar p` on $J$ the genuine one, where `HeckeAlg` is the polynomial ring $\mathbb{Z}[T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ acting through the evaluation map sending the generator at $\ell$ to `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ`. Second, `hMW`, the Mordell–Weil input: the additive group of Galois-fixed points of $J$, i.e. `FixedPoints.addSubgroup` for the above action, is finitely generated. The conclusion is that, with respect to the module structure `heckeModuleBar p`, the additive subgroup of the Eisenstein quotient generated by the set `eisensteinQuotientRational p (heckeModuleBar p)` is a finitely generated additive group. Here the Eisenstein quotient is $J / \big(\gamma \cdot J\big)$, where $\gamma =$ `eisensteinKernel` of the Eisenstein ideal `eisensteinIdeal p` (the kernel of evaluation of `HeckeAlg` at the system $T_\ell \mapsto 1+\ell$ for $\ell \nmid p$, $T_p \mapsto 1$), $\gamma$ consisting of those $t$ for which there is $i$ in the Eisenstein ideal with $(1+i)t$ annihilating all of $J$; and `eisensteinQuotientRational` is the image in that quotient of the set of $x \in J$ with $\sigma \cdot x - x \in \gamma \cdot J$ for every $\sigma$ in the Galois group. Thus the statement asserts finite generation of the subgroup generated by the classically named group $\tilde{J}(\mathbb{Q})$; the passage to the generated subgroup is a wrapper, since that set is already closed under addition.
--
--   This is the finite-generation half of the assertion that the rational part of Mazur's Eisenstein quotient of $J_0(p)$ is finite, as in Mazur's study of the Eisenstein ideal. Unlike the textbook treatment, no theory of abelian varieties or of quotients by optimal isogenies is used: the Eisenstein quotient is formed inside the divisor class group as the quotient by $\gamma \cdot J$ for the explicit annihilator ideal $\gamma$, and Mordell–Weil enters only as the hypothesis that the Galois-fixed subgroup of $J$ is finitely generated. Together with the companion statement that every element of `eisensteinQuotientRational` is torsion, it yields the finiteness of the rational part of the Eisenstein quotient, `eisensteinQuotientInvariantsFiniteAt_heckeModuleBar`, which is the finiteness input for the Mazur step-three package.
